# Policy As Data: The Lethal Trifecta

[![Run in Livebook](https://livebook.dev/badge/v1/blue.svg)](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Frefpath%2Fsigil_guard%2Fblob%2Fmain%2Fnotebooks%2Fpolicy-and-lethal-trifecta.livemd)

The dangerous agent shape is not “an LLM with tools.” It is an agent that can
read private data, consume untrusted instructions, and communicate externally
in the same flow. Remove any one edge and the risk drops sharply. Leave all
three connected and a prompt injection can become an exfiltration path.

SigilGuard turns those edges into ordinary, reviewable policy data. This
chapter compiles one policy file, exercises its boundary rules, applies its
repository rules, and enforces its outbound data contract.

## Setup

```elixir
repo_root = Path.expand("..", __DIR__)
local_checkout? = File.regular?(Path.join(repo_root, "mix.exs"))

{sigil_guard_dep, install_opts} =
  if local_checkout? do
    {
      {:sigil_guard, path: repo_root, env: :prod},
      [
        config_path: Path.join(repo_root, "config/config.exs"),
        lockfile: Path.join(repo_root, "mix.lock")
      ]
    }
  else
    {{:sigil_guard, "~> 1.0"}, []}
  end

Mix.install([sigil_guard_dep], install_opts)
```

## One File, Three Kinds Of Policy

The `[rules]` section governs runtime boundaries. `[repo]` governs proposed
file changes. `[contracts]` constrains content that is otherwise allowed to
cross an outbound sink. These are separate jobs, but keeping them in one
signed and reviewable artifact makes the authority legible.

```elixir
policy_text = """
version 3

[rules]
default allow
block sensitivity:private origin:tool,resource,repo zone:untrusted sink:external,network trust:low,medium
confirm sensitivity:private origin:tool,resource,repo zone:untrusted sink:external,network trust:high
allow sensitivity:public sink:model

[repo]
default require_approval
allow agent:spiffe://demo.example/agents/docs action:modify docs/** README.md
block agent:* priv/secrets/**

[contracts]
contract sink:external,network max_size:96 no_raw_credentials:true digest_only_pii:true classes:text credential_transform:mask
"""

{:ok, policy} = SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.File.parse(policy_text)

64 = String.length(policy.digest)
3 = length(policy.rules)
true = not is_nil(policy.repo)
true = Map.has_key?(policy.contracts, "external")

%{
  digest: policy.digest,
  boundary_rules: length(policy.rules),
  repo_rules: length(policy.repo.rules),
  contract_sinks: policy.contracts |> Map.keys() |> Enum.sort()
}
```

The digest is over the raw policy bytes. A comment, newline, or rule edit is a
different reviewed artifact even when two parsed structures happen to behave
the same.

## Make The Three Edges Explicit

Here is the risky path: a tool supplied private content, the content came from
an untrusted zone, and the agent wants to send it outside the system.

```elixir
digest = String.duplicate("a", 64)

trifecta = [
  phase: :model_egress,
  source: :tool,
  origin: :tool,
  sink: :external,
  source_sensitivity: :private,
  trust_zone: :untrusted,
  trust_level: :medium,
  action_digest: digest,
  payload_digest: digest,
  context_digest: digest
]

blocked = SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.evaluate(trifecta, policy: policy)

:block = blocked.action
true = Enum.any?(blocked.matched_rules, &(&1.rule_id == "line_5"))

%{action: blocked.action, reason: blocked.reason, rules: blocked.matched_rules}
```

> **On stage:** draw the three edges before evaluating. The point is not the
> phrase “lethal trifecta”; it is that every edge is now a typed policy input.

## Trust Changes The Workflow, Not The Facts

A high-trust actor does not turn private, untrusted content into public data.
The policy changes the workflow from a hard block to exact human approval.

```elixir
high_trust = Keyword.put(trifecta, :trust_level, :high)
approval_required = SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.evaluate(high_trust, policy: policy)

:confirm = approval_required.action

%{action: approval_required.action, reason: approval_required.reason}
```

Remove one edge and the matching rule no longer applies. This variant sends
public content back to the model, not to an external system.

```elixir
public_model_boundary =
  trifecta
  |> Keyword.put(:sink, :model)
  |> Keyword.put(:source_sensitivity, :public)
  |> Keyword.put(:trust_zone, :semi_trusted)

allowed = SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.evaluate(public_model_boundary, policy: policy)

:allow = allowed.action
%{action: allowed.action, reason: allowed.reason}
```

## Repository Changes Use A Path Kernel

Repository governance has a smaller vocabulary: allow, require approval, or
block. Paths are normalized and unsafe relative paths fail before rule
matching. A documentation agent can edit docs, but it cannot wander into a
secret-bearing directory.

```elixir
docs_change =
  SigilGuard.RepoPolicy.evaluate(policy.repo,
    agent: "spiffe://demo.example/agents/docs",
    action: "modify",
    changed_paths: ["docs/architecture.md", "README.md"]
  )

secret_change =
  SigilGuard.RepoPolicy.evaluate(policy.repo,
    agent: "spiffe://demo.example/agents/docs",
    action: "modify",
    changed_paths: ["priv/secrets/provider.key"]
  )

unmatched_change =
  SigilGuard.RepoPolicy.evaluate(policy.repo,
    agent: "spiffe://demo.example/agents/docs",
    action: "modify",
    changed_paths: ["lib/payment.ex"]
  )

:allow = docs_change.verdict
:block = secret_change.verdict
:require_approval = unmatched_change.verdict

%{
  docs: docs_change.verdict,
  secrets: secret_change.verdict,
  unmatched: unmatched_change.verdict,
  unmatched_paths: unmatched_change.unmatched_paths
}
```

The repo decision can be folded into a boundary decision without inventing a
second policy language at the adapter seam.

```elixir
repo_facts = SigilGuard.RepoPolicy.policy_facts(policy.repo, unmatched_change)

repo_boundary = [
  phase: :model_egress,
  source: :model,
  origin: :model,
  sink: :repo,
  trust_zone: :trusted,
  trust_level: :high,
  action_digest: digest,
  payload_digest: digest,
  context_digest: digest
]

repo_gate =
  SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.evaluate(repo_boundary,
    policy: policy,
    repo_facts: repo_facts
  )

:confirm = repo_gate.action
%{action: repo_gate.action, repo: unmatched_change.verdict}
```

## Allowed Does Not Mean Unconstrained

An output contract runs only after policy permits a crossing. It can restrict
the content class, replace raw credentials, hash designated PII, and cap the
final UTF-8 byte size. The host supplies the credential and PII spans from its
approved detectors.

```elixir
alias SigilGuard.BoundaryPolicy.Contract

contract = policy.contracts["external"]
credential = "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE"
email = "operator@example.test"

outbound =
  "Build owner #{email}; credential #{credential}; " <>
    String.duplicate("diagnostic ", 12)

{:ok, constrained} =
  Contract.enforce(contract, "external", outbound,
    verdict: :allow,
    credentials: [credential],
    pii: [email]
  )

false = String.contains?(constrained, credential)
false = String.contains?(constrained, email)
true = byte_size(constrained) <= 96
true = String.ends_with?(constrained, "[TRUNCATED]")

%{bytes: byte_size(constrained), output: constrained}
```

A structured payload is blocked here because this contract admits only text.
There is no lossy conversion to “make it fit.”

```elixir
{:block, "contract.external.class"} =
  Contract.enforce(contract, "external", %{"status" => "ok"}, verdict: :allow)
```

## Takeaway

Policy is doing three different forms of work: deciding whether a boundary may
be crossed, deciding which repository paths an agent may change, and
constraining the bytes that leave after an allow. Keeping those jobs explicit
is what makes the result deterministic and auditable.

